…receiving them with proper ceremony and winning their allegiance through appropriate means: this is how the states are brought into amity. When circumstances make punitive expeditions unavoidable, military affairs are instituted: those who transgress must be punished, and those who refuse to attend court are attacked. This is how the states are pacified. Do these two branches of government not occupy the seventh and eighth places? Thus the application of the eight branches of government has an order according to urgency. If the ruler undertakes them in sequence, the people's livelihood will flourish. How, then, could the august standard remain unestablished and the constant human relationships remain unordered? Broadly speaking, the essential task of kingly government is to enrich the people's lives. Food, wealth, and dwelling provide the means of sustaining life; education brings their lives to fulfillment; sacrifices and the reception of guests establish relations with spirits and human beings, thereby aiding their lives; punishments and military affairs eliminate the disobedient, thereby protecting their lives. By following Heaven and bringing the people to fulfillment, the way of government becomes complete. Yet the august standard is the foundation of the eight branches of government. If the ruler cultivates virtue and establishes this standard above, and also appoints officials to lead their subordinates in administering their respective responsibilities, government will reach perfection. Hence it is said: “Those who secure the state's foundation prize equitable government; those who strengthen the eight branches of government take the august standard as their foundation.”

Book of Odes
“As if poised on tiptoe, stately;
as if an arrow, straight;
as if a bird spreading its wings;
as if a pheasant taking flight:
there the lord ascends.”
Ou Xiji
Associate Examiner Chen, Instructor, comments: “The description of the scale and design of the construction is vivid and apt. The atmosphere of the restoration of that time appears distinctly before our eyes.”
Associate Examiner Wu, Instructor, comments: “The grandeur and beauty of the royal construction cannot readily be expressed in words…”
